Here's what I found by searching the indexed `w1-28` AL source and docs.

## Core AL objects for outbound REST calls

Business Central's AL language exposes a first-class set of HTTP data types (not a System App wrapper module in this w1-28 build — I checked for the newer `System.RestClient` codeunit and it isn't present in this version, so the base types below are what's actually used everywhere):

| Type | Role |
|---|---|
| `HttpClient` | Makes the call — `.Get()`, `.Post()`, `.Send()` |
| `HttpRequestMessage` | Method, URI, headers, body |
| `HttpResponseMessage` | Status code, headers, response content |
| `HttpContent` | Request/response body (JSON, text, etc.) |
| `HttpHeaders` | Headers on either the request or the content |

## Real call-site example: sending mail via Microsoft Graph

`codeunit 4508 "Email - Outlook API Client"` (`w1-28-src/Email - Outlook REST API/src/EmailOutlookAPIClient.Codeunit.al`) is a clean, complete example — it POSTs to the Graph API to send an email:

```al
local procedure SendMailSingleRequest(AccessToken: SecretText; MessageJson: JsonObject)
var
    MailHttpContent: HttpContent;
    MailHttpRequestMessage: HttpRequestMessage;
    MailHttpResponseMessage: HttpResponseMessage;
    MailRequestHeaders: HttpHeaders;
    MailContentHeaders: HttpHeaders;
    MailHttpClient: HttpClient;
    MessageJsonText: Text;
    HttpErrorMessage: Text;
    RequestUri: Text;
begin
    MessageJson.WriteTo(MessageJsonText);
    RequestUri := GraphURLTxt + '/v1.0/me/sendMail';   // GraphURLTxt = 'https://graph.microsoft.com'

    MailHttpRequestMessage.Method('POST');
    MailHttpRequestMessage.SetRequestUri(RequestUri);
    MailHttpRequestMessage.GetHeaders(MailRequestHeaders);
    MailRequestHeaders.Add('Authorization', SecretStrSubstNo('Bearer %1', AccessToken));

    MailHttpContent.WriteFrom(MessageJsonText);
    MailHttpContent.GetHeaders(MailContentHeaders);
    MailContentHeaders.Clear();
    MailContentHeaders.Add('Content-Type', 'application/json');

    MailHttpRequestMessage.Content := MailHttpContent;

    if not MailHttpClient.Send(MailHttpRequestMessage, MailHttpResponseMessage) then
        if MailHttpResponseMessage.IsBlockedByEnvironment() then
            Error(EnvironmentBlocksErr)
        else
            Error(ConnectionErr);

    if MailHttpResponseMessage.HttpStatusCode <> 202 then begin
        HttpErrorMessage := GetHttpErrorMessageAsText(MailHttpResponseMessage);
        ...
    end;
end;
```
(`EmailOutlookAPIClient.Codeunit.al:599-636`)

Key patterns worth reusing:
- **`IsBlockedByEnvironment()`** — checks whether outgoing HTTP is disabled for the extension (also seen in the error label: *"enable outgoing HTTP requests for the ... app on the Extension Management page"*). Always check this — it's the #1 real-world gotcha for outbound calls.
- A shared `CreateRequest(Method, RequestUri, AccessToken, var HttpRequestMessage)` + `SendRequest(var Request, var Response)` helper pair (lines ~320-341) is used across ~20 procedures in this codeunit for GET/POST/PATCH calls — a good template for your own connector.
- Simple **GET** pattern is even shorter, using `HttpClient.Get()` directly instead of building a request message:
```al
AccountRequestHeaders := AccountHttpClient.DefaultRequestHeaders();
AccountRequestHeaders.Add('Authorization', SecretStrSubstNo('Bearer %1', AccessToken));
if not AccountHttpClient.Get(GraphURLTxt + '/v1.0/me', AccountResponseMessage) then
    exit;
AccountResponseMessage.Content().ReadAs(ResponseContent);
```

## Other real usages for comparison

- **`E-Document Core`** interface `IDocumentResponseHandler` / `IDocumentSender` (`w1-28-src/E-Document Core/src/Integration/EDocumentIntegration.Interface.al`) — the standard integration pattern for e-invoicing connectors, taking `HttpRequestMessage`/`HttpResponseMessage` params and auto-logging them.
- **`SignUp Authentication`** (`E-Document Connector - SignUp/src/SignUpAuthentication.Codeunit.al`) — OAuth client-credentials flow error handling around `HttpResponseMessage`.
- **`AFS Optional Parameters`** / **`ABS Optional Parameters`** (Azure File/Blob Services API) — header-builder helper pattern via `SetRequestHeader`.

## Documentation
No dev-itpro AL language reference pages for `HttpClient` turned up in the indexed docs (this index currently holds the functional/admin `business-central` docs, not the `dev-itpro` developer reference — worth checking whether that subtree got indexed for your PoC). This is itself a useful data point for the two-layer PoC: the codebase search nailed real call sites and interfaces, but for the "how do I use HttpClient" conceptual question you'd currently need the docs+code combined index to actually include `dev-itpro`.
